WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200 line:8% position:50% align:center
During my postdoctoral research, I met a professor at a university in Delaware.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
He was developing a new kind of powered mobility device.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.300 line:8% position:50% align:center
The problem was that it was far too expensive.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
That summer, he began looking for a lower-cost approach.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
He repurposed a ride-on toy car and adapted the concept.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.800 line:8% position:50% align:center
The child in that case was over two years old and already had a paediatric power wheelchair.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:34.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
It offered excellent support and positioning, but it was also a very expensive option.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.500 line:8% position:50% align:center
It was large and heavy. If it was not charged, it could not be moved easily.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
At school, it also needed a certain amount of space to manoeuvre.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:51.500 line:8% position:50% align:center
During tabletop activities, the wheelchair could become a constraint; the child could only use its own tray.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.400 line:8% position:50% align:center
When teachers led group activities, the child often stayed passively in one place.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:03.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
And, to be honest, its appearance did not feel especially approachable. I still remember one scene clearly.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
There was an outdoor play area beside the kindergarten.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.400 line:8% position:50% align:center
Whenever the wheelchair approached, the other children moved aside so it could pass.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:21.000 line:8% position:50% align:center
Later, we modified a toy car for the child. The children nearby immediately gathered around.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.200 line:8% position:50% align:center
Everyone wanted to press its buttons and touch its rear-view mirror.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
For children that age, appearance matters. We had chosen a Lightning McQueen model.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.100 line:8% position:50% align:center
It felt much more approachable to them.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:43.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
At first, many parents hope their child will walk independently without relying on any external device.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.800 line:8% position:50% align:center
This child had severe cerebral palsy, high muscle tone and very restricted movement.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:56.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
They went for a wheelchair fitting. His mother knew that he needed this kind of assistive device.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:03.400 line:8% position:50% align:center
But when her child actually sat in the wheelchair, she cried. The emotional impact was much greater.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
It felt like she was telling herself, “My child has a disability.”

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:13.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
I am not trying to replace the function of a power wheelchair with a toy car.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.100 line:8% position:50% align:center
But this kind of toy can reduce the child’s reliance on being pushed or carried.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:24.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
It lets the child move independently and may make a later transition to a conventional aid easier to accept.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.800 line:8% position:50% align:center
I genuinely think the concept is very good. I have worked in this field for many years.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:36.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
Research has shown me the limitations, including rapid growth and the different positioning needs of each child.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.300 line:8% position:50% align:center
Your current design has taken those postural adjustments into account.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
If it can deliver all the functions we discussed, I have high expectations for it.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
For the next step, however, I would recommend reducing the weight.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:59.200 line:8% position:50% align:center
That is why we originally used a ride-on toy car. It is very light and easy to carry.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
You may need to reconsider the material to achieve that.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:12.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
About two weeks after birth, he was diagnosed with laryngomalacia.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:18.400 line:8% position:50% align:center
He had surgery at around two months old. It was only after he left hospital...

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
...that we could have close physical contact. In the neonatal ward, the babies were kept tightly swaddled.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:34.400 line:8% position:50% align:center
The hospital staff could not spend a great deal of time holding and playing with every child individually.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:40.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
At three months, I realised that he still could not lift his head or grasp objects.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.800 line:8% position:50% align:center
His hands had very little strength, and he could not roll over.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.200 line:8% position:50% align:center
We began physical therapy when he was just over four months old.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:54.000 line:8% position:50% align:center
Only then did he slowly begin learning to roll, crawl and sit.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.900 line:8% position:50% align:center
At the time, he could not sit. If we placed him upright, he fell over and could not support his body.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:04.300 line:8% position:50% align:center
He is one now. At that age, children can usually walk while holding on to furniture.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:09.100 line:8% position:50% align:center
He can pull himself up and stand, but then he cannot move.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:14.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
He wants to walk, but his legs may not be strong enough, and he has not yet learnt how to initiate the movement.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.100 line:8% position:50% align:center
That is roughly where he is now. Sit down, then stand again.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
Move forwards a little. Yes.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.000 line:8% position:50% align:center
Keep your hands moving. Keep going.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.700 line:8% position:50% align:center
You’re doing fine. Great job!

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
Relax your shoulders. That’s it.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.500 line:8% position:50% align:center
Well done! That’s brilliant.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.400 line:8% position:50% align:center
Go and find Mum. Mum!

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000 line:8% position:50% align:center
Turn around. Turn around.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.600 line:8% position:50% align:center
For him, it creates more opportunities to adapt to different things...

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.500 line:8% position:50% align:center
...different toys and a wider range of experiences. I think that is a good thing.
